THE RULE THAT MATTERS
Ownership scales BOTH income and deductions.
Days available scales DEDUCTIONS ONLY.

Rent received is rent received. Scaling income down because the property
was off the market for part of the year would understate assessable rent -
an error in the taxpayer's favour. Four tests pin this explicitly:
income is unaffected by availability and by private use, while the same
facts reduce deductions.

Private-use days are subtracted from available days rather than applied as
a second multiplier, because a day the owner occupied the property is not
a day it was available. Applying both as factors would double-discount.

ASSUMPTIONS ARE REPORTED
Absent facts fall back to sole ownership and a full year, and
assumedSoleOwnership / assumedFullYear are returned so the report can say
"(assumed)" rather than presenting a fallback as recorded - the same
distinction enforced elsewhere in this track.

DISPLAY DECISION
Only the summary is apportioned; expense tables keep full amounts, with a
note saying so. Apportioning individual invoice lines would produce
amounts that match no document the agent can check against.

